        "id": "ch6_1_1-1",
        "text": "History of Cinnamon\nThe Cinnamon desktop project was initiated in 2011 by the developers of the Linux\nMint distribution as an alternative to GNOME 3. It uses much of the same back-\nground code as the GNOME 3 desktop project, but keeps the original look-and-feel\nof the GNOME 2 desktop, as shown in Figure 6-1.\nThe Cinnamon desktop in Linux Mint has many similar features to the Windows\ndesktop:\n\u00bb\n\u00bb  A single panel at the bottom of the desktop\n\u00bb\n\u00bb  An application launcher at the left-hand edge of the panel\n\u00bb\n\u00bb  A system tray at the right-hand edge of the panel\n\u00bb\n\u00bb  A desktop area where you can save folders and files\nThe Cinnamon application launcher was designed to be similar to the old GNOME\n2 menu system. I dig deeper into that in the next section.\nFIGURE 6-1:\nThe Cinnamon\ndesktop as seen\nin Linux Mint.\nCHAPTER 6 Exploring Alternative Desktops 103\nYour specific Linux distribution\u2019s Cinnamon desktop may vary slightly from\nwhat\u2019s shown in Figure 6-1. With Cinnamon you can move the panel to any side\nof the desktop, and you can even have multiple panels. Just right-click an empty\nplace on the panel and select the Move option to move the panel, or the Add Panel\noption to create additional panels.",
